Allocate Minimum Number of Pages

(Pattern: Binary Search on Answer)

🎯 Aim

To allocate books to students such that:

Each student gets contiguous books

Every student gets at least one book

The maximum pages assigned to any student is minimized

🔍 Problem Statement

Given:

Array pages[] where pages[i] = number of pages in the i-th book

An integer m = number of students

Return the minimum possible value of the maximum pages assigned to a student.

Example
pages = [12, 34, 67, 90]
students = 2

Output
113


Explanation

Student 1 → [12, 34, 67] = 113

Student 2 → [90] = 90
Max = 113 (minimum possible)

🧠 Key Insight

This is NOT DP

Answer lies between:

max(pages) (minimum possible)

sum(pages) (maximum possible)

👉 This screams Binary Search on Answer

⚙️ Algorithm

If students > number of books, return -1

Set:

low  = max(pages)
high = sum(pages)


Binary search on this range

For each mid, check:

Can we allocate books so that no student gets > mid pages?

If yes → try smaller value

If no → increase value

💻 Java Code (Very Common Interview Code)
class AllocatePages {

    static boolean isPossible(int[] pages, int students, int maxPages) {
        int count = 1;
        int sum = 0;

        for (int p : pages) {
            if (sum + p <= maxPages) {
                sum += p;
            } else {
                count++;
                sum = p;

                if (count > students)
                    return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    static int allocatePages(int[] pages, int students) {

        if (students > pages.length)
            return -1;

        int low = 0, high = 0;

        for (int p : pages) {
            high += p;
            low = Math.max(low, p);
        }

        int ans = -1;

        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (isPossible(pages, students, mid)) {
                ans = mid;
                high = mid - 1;
            } else {
                low = mid + 1;
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] pages = {12, 34, 67, 90};
        int students = 2;

        System.out.println(allocatePages(pages, students));
    }
}

🧾 Output
113

🪜 Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 isPossible()

Checks if allocation can be done with a given max page limit.

Assign books sequentially

When pages exceed maxPages, move to next student

If students exceed limit → return false

🔹 Binary Search Loop
while (low <= high)


Try a mid value

If feasible → try smaller (high = mid - 1)

Else → increase (low = mid + 1)

